# {{repo}}

Articles for one publication: drafts, metadata, hand-offs and `ci-review` reviews, one folder per article. Each round of an article is a branch and a pull request, so `main` holds only accepted text and the history shows what changed in each round, why, and in answer to which review. This repository is private.

The full rules are in [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). This page is the short version for a person.

## Layout

- `articles/<slug>/` holds one article, with fixed file names: `draft.md`, `metadata.md`, `publication.md`, `close-out-to-code.md`, `close-out-to-chat.md`, `review.md` and `pipeline/`. The round and the run are not in the names; they are in git.
- `articles/<slug>/review.md` and `pipeline/` are written by `ci-review` only. `pipeline/` holds the run's report and the worklist, which are for the author.
- `templates/` holds the shapes of those files.
- `instructions/claude-project.md` is pasted at the top of the claude.ai project's instructions. `instructions/publication.md` holds the publication's own drafting rules and belongs to the author.
- `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `.gitattributes`, `articles/README.md`, `templates/` and `instructions/claude-project.md` are written by `ci-docs init` from the content-intelligence repository. Edit them there, not here.

## The round loop

1. The drafting chat creates the branch `round/<slug>/rN` from `main`, commits `draft.md` and `metadata.md` to it in one commit (`draft: <slug> rN`) and opens a pull request titled `<slug> rN`. The description says what changed since the last accepted round, and why.
2. The author runs `ci-review --github <slug> --write-back`. It commits `review.md` and `pipeline/` to the branch and posts one pull request review: a summary and a checklist of the main findings, F1, F2 and so on.
3. The chat reads the review and revises on the same branch. Each revision commit (`revise: <slug> rN after run M`) says which findings it addressed, which it deferred (each deferred one is an issue) and which it did not act on. The author can ask for another review.
4. When the author says so in the chat, the chat merges the pull request with a merge commit. The next command that writes to the article tags the merge `<slug>/rN`. Nothing is tagged by hand.

The branch `round/<slug>/rN` and the tag `<slug>/rN` are different strings on purpose.

## Merge commits only

`ci-docs init` sets this repository to allow merge commits and nothing else. A squash merge would replace a round's draft, review and revision commits with one, and a rebase merge would rewrite them; either loses the history this repository exists to keep. Merged branches are left in place.

## Refreshing the managed files and settings

From a checkout of content-intelligence:

```
uv run ci-docs init --repo {{repo}}
```

It adds what is missing, skips what is identical, replaces a managed file only if nobody has edited it here, and lists the ones that were edited. It also sets the merge-method settings above and creates the issue labels (`round`, `known-gap`, `research`, `decision`, `correction`, `from-review`) when they are missing; it never edits or deletes a label that exists. It never touches an article or `instructions/publication.md`. Add `--dry-run` to see the plan first.
